.TH NIHILURK 6 "September 2026" "nihilurk 0.1.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
nihilurk \- terminal roguelike
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nihilurk
[\fB\-s\fR \fIseed\fR] [\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-ns\fR] [\fB\-nb\fR]
[\fB\-nshake\fR] [\fB\-anim\-rate\fR \fImultiplier\fR] [\fIname\fR | \fIsave\fR]
.PP
.B nihilurk
\fB\-content\fR
.PP
.B nihilurk
\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-help\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nihilurk
is a turn-based terminal roguelike: exploration, combat, and survival in a
thirteen-floor dungeon, drawn on a grid of character cells and answered one
keystroke at a time.
.PP
To win, descend to the thirteenth floor, take the
.I "Element of Yoord"
that waits there, and carry it up and out of the dungeon. Once you carry it,
the only way out is up, and the creatures you meet may be drawn from anywhere
in the dungeon. Few runs come back up. Death is permanent; there is no retry
and no resurrection.
.PP
To get started you only need to know two things: the direction keys move you,
and walking into a monster attacks it. The rest of the commands are listed
below.
.PP
The status line keeps the numbers you need at a glance: health, magic, attack,
defence, depth, and score. The message log beneath the map describes what has
just happened; when
.B \-\-MORE\-\-
appears, press Space or Enter to continue reading.
.PP
The Dungeon Lord allows only so much hesitation on each floor. Stay too long
and a portal carries you to another depth whether you are ready or not.
.PP
The game uses one save file,
.IR name .sav ,
in the directory nihilurk was started from. Quitting saves the current run unless
.B \-ns
is used, and starting the game again with the same name continues it. A run
that ends in death is deleted. A completed run is retained as clear data:
beginning another journey on it asks first, because the Element returns to
the Dungeon Lord, and declining leaves the world its light.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help ", " \-\-help
Print a short command-line guide and exit without entering the alternate
screen. This page is the full reference.
.TP
.BI \-s " seed"
Use
.I seed
as the run's unsigned 64-bit seed. The seed fixes the dungeon's maps: the
walls of a floor are the same every time that floor of that seed is entered,
today, tomorrow, and after a reload. Monsters, items, and traps are rolled
afresh on each entry, so a familiar corridor may hold unfamiliar company, and
two runs on one seed that take the same staircases see the same everything.
An unparseable or missing value is ignored and the seed is drawn at random.
.TP
.B \-c
Centre the map on the player instead of using the fixed viewport.
.TP
.B \-ns
Do not write a save file. The run leaves nothing behind, on quit or on
victory.
.TP
.B \-nb
Disable bloodstains and the corpse-and-bones death animation.
.TP
.B \-nshake
Disable screen shake. The map never leaves its moorings;
.B \-anim\-rate
can only slow a shake, not still one.
.TP
.BI \-anim\-rate " multiplier"
Set the on-screen hold time for particle, mapping, and shake animation
frames. The default is 1.0; raise it for a terminal whose redraw cannot keep
up, lower it for snappier animations. The value is clamped to 0.1 through 5.0;
an invalid or missing value uses the default.
.TP
.B \-content
Print every content name accepted by
.BR NIHILURK_SPAWN ,
grouped by category, and exit without entering the alternate screen, so the
output pipes into
.BR grep (1)
and
.BR less (1)
like any other listing.
.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENT
.TP
.I name
Start a new run under this player name. The name is uppercased in the game,
and the save file is lowercased from it.
.TP
.I save
Load a save file if the argument names an existing file, or if appending
.B .sav
names one. Matching is case-insensitive. If the save is clear data, a won
run, the game asks before spending it.
.PP
An unrecognised argument is treated as the player name, so a typo becomes a
nihilurk rather than an error. Options may appear in any order; a later positional
argument replaces an earlier one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NIHILURK_SPAWN
A comma-separated list of exact content names. Each recognised name is
spawned on the nearest free walkable tile around the player whenever a floor
is built. Empty entries and unknown names are ignored. The setting applies to
every floor, including floors revisited on the way back up. Use
.B \-content
to discover names:
.sp
.EX
nihilurk -content | grep -i dragon
NIHILURK_SPAWN="dragon,bow,arrow" nihilurk
.EE
.TP
.B NIHILURK_MAGICMAP
Select the style of the magic-mapping reveal animation instead of rolling one.
Row by row:
.BR row ,
.BR rows ,
.BR rowbyrow ,
.BR row-by-row ,
and
.BR curtain ;
spiral:
.BR spiral
and
.BR swirl ;
explode:
.BR explode ,
.BR explosion ,
.BR blast ,
and
.BR burst .
An unrecognised value selects a style at random.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
nihilurk
nihilurk bae
nihilurk -s 1234 -ns
nihilurk -content | grep -i dragon
NIHILURK_SPAWN="basilisk" nihilurk
.EE
.SH SPAWN API
The game exposes its content lookup and spawn helpers from the
.B models
crate. They are intended for tests, tools, and engine code rather than normal
play.
.TP
.BI "spawn_named(world, name, pos)"
Returns an entity for the exact, case-sensitive content name, or
.BR None .
It can spawn monsters, items, traps, and the Element of Yoord. A named spawn
uses the catalog row as written: it does not roll loot enchantments, wand
charges, or ammunition bundles.
.TP
.B content_names()
Returns every name accepted by
.BR spawn_named ();
the result is grouped by category and is the source of the
.B \-content
listing.
.TP
.BI "spawn_requested(world, near, occupied)"
Reads
.B NIHILURK_SPAWN
and places recognised names around
.IR near .
It is the convenience wrapper used during floor generation.
.TP
.BI "spawn_list(world, list, near, occupied)"
The testable form of
.BR spawn_requested .
It reads a comma-separated string directly and returns the number of names
recognised.
.PP
For random floor loot, use
.BR roll_item ();
it selects a category and row using the floor's depth and random source, then
applies that row's normal loot generation.
.PP
The Rust API is documented in
.IR docs/reference/spawn-api.md .
.SH GAME CONTROLS
.SS MOVEMENT
Move with the arrow keys, the vi keys, or the number pad. The vi and
number-pad directions are:
.PP
.EX
        y/7    k/8    u/9
        h/4           l/6
        b/1    j/2    n/3
.EE
.PP
Walk into a monster to attack it. Other useful movement commands are:
.TP
.B Shift + direction
Run in that direction until something requires attention.
.TP
.B o
Auto-explore the current floor.
.TP
.B A
Toggle picking up useful items during auto-explore.
.TP
.B O
Choose a destination and travel to it.
.TP
.B L
Look at a tile: read what is there without acting on it.
.TP
.B Tab
Auto-fight the nearest visible threat.
.TP
.B f
Fire a matching arrow or quarrel with the launcher in hand.
.TP
.B v
Make a reach attack with a suitable weapon.
.SS ITEMS AND MENUS
.TP
.B i
Open the pack.
.TP
.BR a ", " t ", " d
Use, throw, or drop an item.
.TP
.B e
Equip wearable or wieldable equipment.
.TP
.BR q ", " r ", " z
Quaff a potion, read a scroll, or zap a wand.
.TP
.BR w ", " W ", " P
Wield a weapon, wear armour, or put on a ring.
.TP
.B Z
Open the moves menu. The
.B "Alt-Q, Alt-W, Alt-E, and Alt-R"
keys fire the four move slots directly.
.SS STAIRS AND EXIT
.TP
.BR "> , ."
Go down, or walk to the down stairs.
.TP
.BR "< , ,"
Go up, or walk to the up stairs.
.TP
.B x
Close the current menu or targeting cursor without spending a turn.
.TP
.B Esc
Back out of a menu. It does not quit.
.TP
.B Q
Quit after confirmation.
.TP
.B X
Close the current interface; with nothing open, quit after confirmation.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Quit immediately.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR name .sav
The single save file for a run named
.IR name ,
in the directory nihilurk was started from. A won run keeps the same file as
clear data.
